117:
(a) On or before January 15 of each year, the county clerk shall prepare a compilation of all resolutions, ordinances or other provisions enacted during the previous year which may amend, repeal, modify or supplement provisions of the county Code.
(b) By contract or by county personnel, supplements to this Code shall be prepared on at least an annual basis. A supplement to the Code shall include all substantive, permanent and general parts of ordinances passed by the board of commissioners of the county during the period covered by the supplement and all changes made thereby in the Code. The pages of a supplement shall be so numbered that they will fit properly into the Code and will, where necessary, replace pages which have become obsolete or partially obsolete, and the new pages shall be so prepared that, when they have been inserted, the Code will be current through the date of the adoption of the latest ordinance included in the supplement.
(c) In preparing a supplement to this Code, all portions of the Code which have been repealed shall be excluded from the Code by the omission thereof from reprinted pages.
(d) When preparing a supplement to this Code, the codifier (meaning the person, agency or organization authorized to prepare the supplement) may make formal, nonsubstantive changes in ordinances and parts of ordinances included in the supplement, insofar as it is necessary to do so to embody them into a unified Code. For example, the codifier may:
(1) Organize the ordinance material into appropriate subdivisions;
(2) Provide appropriate catchlines, headings and titles for sections and other subdivisions of the Code printed in the supplement, and make changes in such catchlines, headings and titles;
(3) Assign appropriate numbers to sections and other subdivisions to be inserted in the Code and, where necessary to accommodate new material, change existing section or other subdivision numbers;
(4) Change the words "this ordinance" or words of the same meaning to "this chapter," "this article," "this division," etc., as the case may be, or to "sections _____ to _____" (inserting section numbers to indicate the sections of the Code which embody the substantive sections of the ordinance incorporated into the Code); and
(5) Make other nonsubstantive changes necessary to preserve the original meanings of ordinance sections inserted into the Code; but in no case shall the codifier make any change in the meaning or effect of ordinance material included in the supplement or already embodied in the Code.
(Code 1985, § 1-11)

387:
The governing authority may determine, under existing ordinances or by evidence collected from the board of health or its designee, that a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial or business use if they find that conditions exist in such a dwelling, building or structure, which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwelling, buildings or structures; or other residents of the county. Such conditions may include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accident or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects; and
(6) Uncleanliness.
(Ord. of 7-1-03)

388:
The governing body is authorized to exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purposes of this article, including the following powers in addition to others herein granted:
(1) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(2) To appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents and employees as they deem necessary to carry out the purposes of the ordinances;
(3) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the unincorporated area of the county in order to determine which dwellings, buildings or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial or business use;
(4) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; however, such entry shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession; and
(5) To delegate any of their functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as they may designate.
(Ord. of 7-1-03)

539:
The members of the advisory board shall consist of the following persons:
(1) The sheriff of the county, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(2) The chief of police of the City of Newnan, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(3) The chief of police of the City of Senoia, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(4) The chief of police of the City of Grantville, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(5) The fire chief of the Coweta County Fire Rescue, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(6) The fire chief of the Newnan Fire Department, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(7) A named public safety representative from the City of Palmetto;
(8) A named public safety representative from the City of Chattahoochee Hills;
(9) The post commander for Georgia State Patrol, Post 24, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(10) The Coweta County 911/EMA Director, or his/her named designee from same agency;
(11) The Coweta County Administrator, or his/her named designee from same department;
(12) A city manager from a municipality within Coweta County (e.g., Newnan, Senoia, Grantville), or his/her named designee from said municipality;
(13) A Coweta County citizen appointed by majority decision of the 911 advisory board.
(Ord. No. 019-25, 6-17-25)
